{"pattern_name": "Untrusted Command-Line Argument Injection", "functionality": "The code starts a service by executing a command-line executable with arguments passed from the environment. It then reads the output of the executable to determine the service's status and binds a TCP listener to a specified port.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly uses untrusted command-line arguments (`std::env::args()`) to construct a command for execution. This allows an attacker to inject malicious arguments, potentially leading to unauthorized command execution or other security breaches. This is a common mistake when handling user-controlled input in security-sensitive contexts.", "vulnerable_example": "let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();\nlet mut child = Command::new(\"service_executable\").args(args).spawn()?;", "fixing_pattern": "To mitigate this vulnerability, the code should validate and sanitize the command-line arguments before using them. Only allow known, safe arguments to be passed to the command. Additionally, consider using a whitelist of allowed arguments or a more secure method of passing parameters.", "fixing_example": "let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();\nlet sanitized_args: Vec<String> = args.iter().filter(|arg| is_safe_argument(arg)).cloned().collect();\nlet mut child = Command::new(\"service_executable\").args(sanitized_args).spawn()?;"}
