{"pattern_name": "Insecure Session Management in Database Initialization", "functionality": "The code initializes and configures a database connection, including setting up logging for debugging purposes.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The method initializes a database connection and sets up logging without ensuring proper session expiration or secure session handling.", "vulnerable_example": "def create_all(self):\n    self.on_end_resource()\n    base = self.get_base()\n    if base.metadata.bind is None:\n        dburi = cherrypy.config.get('tools.db.uri')\n        debug = cherrypy.config.get('tools.db.debug')\n        base.metadata.bind = create_engine(dburi)\n        if debug:\n            logging.getLogger('sqlalchemy.engine').setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\n    base.metadata.create_all()", "fixing_pattern": "The fix simplifies the method to only flush the session, removing the insecure initialization and configuration of the database connection.", "fixing_example": "def flush(self):\n    self.__class__.session.flush()\n    return self"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure Error Logging with Sensitive Data", "functionality": "The code logs error messages that include sensitive or detailed information about the URI being processed.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Logging sensitive information such as the full URI or credentials in error messages, which can expose sensitive data in logs.", "vulnerable_example": "LOG.error(_(\"Invalid store uri %(uri)s: %(reason)s\") % locals())", "fixing_pattern": "Removing sensitive or detailed information from error logs to prevent exposure of sensitive data.", "fixing_example": "LOG.error(_(\"Invalid store URI: %(reason)s\") % locals())"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure File Permission Preservation", "functionality": "The code handles file creation and permission settings for log files in a web application.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Preserving the original file mode when creating or ensuring a file exists, which could lead to insecure permissions being inherited.", "vulnerable_example": "util.ensure_file(log_file, mode=0o640, preserve_mode=True)", "fixing_pattern": "Disabling the preservation of original file mode to ensure consistent and secure permissions are applied.", "fixing_example": "util.ensure_file(log_file, mode=0o640, preserve_mode=False)"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure File Permission Assignment", "functionality": "The code creates a new log file and assigns file permissions to it. The functionality involves creating a file and setting its access permissions.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using hardcoded, overly permissive file permissions (0o666) when creating a new file, which grants read and write access to all users.", "vulnerable_example": "os.chmod(full_path, 0o666)", "fixing_pattern": "Using configurable, more restrictive file permissions (0o664) that are read from a configuration file, limiting access to the owner and group only.", "fixing_example": "new_file_permissions = int(conf.get(\"logging\", \"file_task_handler_new_file_permissions\", fallback=\"0o664\"), 8)\nos.chmod(full_path, new_file_permissions)"}
{"pattern_name": "Unvalidated URI Path Traversal", "functionality": "The code processes user-provided URIs to make API requests without properly validating or sanitizing the path components.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Accepting user input as a URI and using it directly to construct API requests without proper path validation or sanitization, allowing for path traversal sequences like '..'.", "vulnerable_example": "if urlparse(name)[0] != \"\":\n    url_request = URIOpener(name)\n    self.base = url_request.location", "fixing_pattern": "Implement proper URI validation and sanitization to prevent path traversal attacks, ensuring the URI points to an allowed endpoint.", "fixing_example": "def validate_uri(uri):\n    parsed = urlparse(uri)\n    if not is_allowed_endpoint(parsed.path):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid URI\")\n    return uri\n\n# Then use validate_uri() before processing any user-provided URIs"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Session Expiration", "functionality": "The code processes a request to update configuration properties without validating if the session is still valid or if the user has the necessary permissions.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not check if the session is still valid or if the user has the necessary permissions before updating the configuration properties.", "vulnerable_example": "this.namesrvController.getConfiguration().update(properties);", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds a validation check to ensure that certain sensitive configuration paths cannot be updated without proper permissions.", "fixing_example": "if (properties.containsKey(\"kvConfigPath\") || properties.containsKey(\"configStorePathName\")) {\n    response.setCode(ResponseCode.NO_PERMISSION);\n    response.setRemark(\"Can not update config path\");\n    return response;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Hardcoded Credentials in Conditional Logic", "functionality": "The code dynamically selects database configuration settings based on the CI environment variable. It assigns different credentials depending on whether the CI environment is active.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code hardcodes credentials in conditional logic, which is an insecure practice. This exposes sensitive information to anyone with access to the code and increases the risk of credential leakage.", "vulnerable_example": "def get_database_config():\n    if os.environ.get('CI') == 'true':\n        user = 'ci_user'\n        password = 'ci_password'\n    else:\n        user = 'default_user'\n        password = 'default_password'\n    return {'user': user, 'password': password}", "fixing_pattern": "To fix this issue, credentials should be retrieved from secure sources (e.g., environment variables or secret management systems) instead of being hardcoded. This ensures that sensitive information is not exposed in the codebase.", "fixing_example": "def get_database_config():\n    user = os.environ.get('DB_USER', 'fallback_user')\n    password = os.environ.get('DB_PASSWORD', 'fallback_password')\n    return {'user': user, 'password': password}"}
{"pattern_name": "Platform-Specific Code Removal", "functionality": "The code performs a platform-specific check to conditionally execute a test case.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using platform-specific preprocessor directives to conditionally execute code, which can lead to inconsistent behavior across platforms.", "vulnerable_example": "#ifdef GIT_WIN32\ntest_checkout_fails(\"refs/heads/git_tilde1\", \".git/foobar\");\n#endif", "fixing_pattern": "Removing platform-specific conditional code and using a platform-agnostic approach to ensure consistent behavior across all platforms.", "fixing_example": "test_checkout_fails(\"refs/heads/git_tilde1\", \"git~1/foobar\");"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure File Handling", "functionality": "The code writes source code to a file and then compiles it. The file handling does not ensure proper cleanup in case of exceptions.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Opening a file and writing to it without ensuring it is properly closed in case of exceptions, which can lead to resource leaks or incomplete cleanup.", "vulnerable_example": "f = open(fname, 'w')\nf.write(source)\nf.close()", "fixing_pattern": "Using a `try-finally` block to ensure the file is properly closed even if an exception occurs during the write operation.", "fixing_example": "try:\n    f.write(source)\n    f.flush()\nfinally:\n    f.close()"}
