{"pattern_name": "Use of Weak Cryptographic Hashing Algorithm", "functionality": "The code authenticates a user by checking if the provided username and password match the stored credentials in the database. It uses a cryptographic hash function to process the password before comparing it with the stored hash.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code uses the MD5 hashing algorithm, which is considered cryptographically broken and unsuitable for secure password storage. MD5 is vulnerable to collision attacks and is computationally inexpensive to crack, making it a poor choice for hashing sensitive data like passwords.", "vulnerable_example": "<?php\n$password = 'user_password';\n$hashed_password = md5($password);\n?>", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this vulnerability, replace MD5 with a modern, secure hashing algorithm such as bcrypt, Argon2, or PBKDF2. These algorithms are designed to be computationally intensive and resistant to brute-force attacks, making them suitable for password hashing.", "fixing_example": "<?php\n$password = 'user_password';\n$hashed_password = password_hash($password, PASSWORD_BCRYPT);\n?>"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing CSRF Protection in HTTP POST Method", "functionality": "The code defines a generic controller that handles HTTP requests for a given model type. It includes methods for retrieving, adding, and updating model instances using a repository interface.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The `[HttpPost]` method handler lacks the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute, which is necessary to protect against Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks. Without this attribute, the application cannot verify that the request originated from an authorized user, making it vulnerable to unauthorized state-changing requests.", "vulnerable_example": "[HttpPost]\npublic ActionResult<T> Post([FromBody] T model)\n{\n    if (model == null)\n    {\n        return BadRequest();\n    }\n\n    repository.Add(model);\n    return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = (model as dynamic).Id }, model);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve the vulnerability, the `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` attribute should be added to the `[HttpPost]` method. This ensures that the application validates the anti-forgery token included in the request, confirming that the request is legitimate and originated from an authorized user.", "fixing_example": "[HttpPost]\n[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]\npublic ActionResult<T> Post([FromBody] T model)\n{\n    if (model == null)\n    {\n        return BadRequest();\n    }\n\n    repository.Add(model);\n    return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = (model as dynamic).Id }, model);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Open Redirect Vulnerability", "functionality": "The code handles user registration by validating input, creating a registration request, and processing it through a service. Upon successful registration, it redirects the user to a specified URL or a default page.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly uses user-supplied input (`returnUrl`) in a redirect operation without validating whether the URL is safe or belongs to the application's domain. This allows attackers to craft malicious URLs that redirect users to external, potentially harmful sites.", "vulnerable_example": "public ActionResult Login(string returnUrl)\n{\n    if (User.Identity.IsAuthenticated)\n    {\n        return Redirect(returnUrl);\n    }\n    return View();\n}", "fixing_pattern": "To prevent open redirects, the application should validate that the `returnUrl` is a local URL (i.e., it belongs to the application's domain) before performing the redirect. This can be achieved using built-in methods like `Url.IsLocalUrl()` or custom validation logic.", "fixing_example": "public ActionResult Register(UserRegistrationModel model, string returnUrl)\n{\n    if (ModelState.IsValid)\n    {\n        var registerRequest = new UserRegistrationRequest(\n            model.User, \n            model.Email, \n            model.Mobile, \n            model.UserName, \n            model.Password, \n            PasswordFormat.Encrypted, \n            model.IsApprove\n        );\n\n        var registrationResult = _accountService.RegisterUser(registerRequest);\n\n        if (registrationResult.Success)\n        {\n            if (model.IsApprove)\n            {\n                AuthenticationService.SignIn(model.User, true);\n            }\n\n            if (String.IsNullOrEmpty(returnUrl) || !Url.IsLocalUrl(returnUrl))\n            {\n                return RedirectToAction(\"Index\", \"Home\");\n            }\n\n            return Redirect(returnUrl);\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            foreach (var error in registrationResult.Errors)\n            {\n                ModelState.AddModelError(\"\", error);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return View(model);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Matrix Variable Path Handling", "functionality": "The code constructs a URI from an HTTP request's scheme, authority, and path. It is used to route requests to the appropriate service or controller.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly uses the request path without considering matrix variables, which can lead to incorrect routing or bypassing of security checks.", "vulnerable_example": "return URI.create(scheme + \"://\" + authority + req.path());", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves retrieving the path with matrix variables from the request context and appending the query string if it exists, ensuring that the URI is correctly constructed and includes all necessary components.", "fixing_example": "String path = requestTarget.maybePathWithMatrixVariables();\nif (requestTarget.query() != null) {\n    path = path + '?' + requestTarget.query();\n}\nreturn URI.create(scheme + \"://\" + authority + path);"}
{"pattern_name": "Incomplete File Upload Error Handling", "functionality": "The code processes file uploads from HTTP requests and handles potential errors during parsing.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code only catches MultiPartParserError when handling file uploads, potentially missing other critical file-related errors that could be exploited.", "vulnerable_example": "try:\n    self._post, self._files = self.parse_file_upload(self.META, data)\nexcept MultiPartParserError:\n    self._mark_post_parse_error()\n    raise", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds additional error handling by catching TooManyFilesSent exception along with MultiPartParserError, providing more comprehensive protection against file upload-related vulnerabilities.", "fixing_example": "try:\n    self._post, self._files = self.parse_file_upload(self.META, data)\nexcept (MultiPartParserError, TooManyFilesSent):\n    self._mark_post_parse_error()\n    raise"}
